#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129967} :activity/id #uuid "df9a25dc-813f-52ef-bbe9-65001555ffea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050324 :topic/id #uuid "3ffd42a4-cf3d-5ec4-9be0-19d427299cac" :topic/math-academy-id 2014} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130348 :tutorial/content "Recall that the matrices $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T} : V \\to V$ relative to two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V$ are different.\n\nIf we are given the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ how do we find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}?$\nTo find $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ we use the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nThe diagram below is a commutative diagram that shows how a change of basis works at each step.\n\n![](images/df/dff02d9f46eb7ded23f418765a50dd1971145cd0e585a82beeef1a45812df8a7.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. So algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor instance, if $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we use this formula to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the formula for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Alternatively, if we know the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\to {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}$ from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B},$ we can construct a similar diagram:\n\n![](images/d6/d6cd195ccd42804ea91debabd23563bb0f6903fdd1b5d708b712c75417fff2a9.png)\n\nFrom this, we can derive the equation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} =\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "daf756b8-28df-5435-a304-0c32de77cc02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3585 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "997bad3a-19a8-5b8c-ba06-b960fea4fabc" :step/math-academy-id 20144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128941 :question/id #uuid "76ff3ea8-e07c-5dba-a9af-a4f68b03fadb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395236}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a9f19301-a016-578d-9241-cc7262d6f30f" :step/math-academy-id 20145 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130349 :tutorial/content "Consider the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{c}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}.$\nThen the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} =P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change of basis matrix from ${\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nWhen written in expanded form, this formula becomes:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ \\,[T(\\,{c}_{1}\\,)]_{C} & \\,\\,\\,\\,⋯\\,\\,\\,\\, & [T(\\,{c}_{n}\\,)]_{C}\\, \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7eacd50-63e4-53d3-9c15-d3a1b6a42ad1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3586 :tutorial/title "The Expanded Form of the Change of Basis"} :step/id #uuid "22fcb777-1e74-5081-b738-034b4f36efdc" :step/math-academy-id 20146 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128942 :question/id #uuid "4b287ac7-7569-59bc-a319-0e62996083bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395243}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "ec690891-eb4f-5c61-8dfc-bd14969e4bcd" :step/math-academy-id 20147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128973 :question/id #uuid "5e4b6303-8ec5-5ba6-ba1d-3b28ff8924b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5897"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395263}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"} :step/id #uuid "b6ae143c-69c0-50ab-a964-29c6f8f34d83" :step/math-academy-id 21621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129032 :question/id #uuid "c269a1d0-a47c-5f5c-96d8-4da2ecd35a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8563"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360750}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"} :step/id #uuid "1481fd15-5d4d-5662-9141-ebd88670e6fc" :step/math-academy-id 31802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129968}}] :activity/title "Connections Between Matrix Representations of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129357}]}
